   "content": [
      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues our study of division by four, so we recommend reading the previous text (<htcite0>) before proceeding. There you will find the ideas on which this lesson is built.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "320a8e1c-0444-4b6e-b202-e82702db329f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "The four divisions",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nAs we have seen, to divide means to transform matter into something else without losing any material, even though the parts end up separated. This principle is called the Law of Conservation of Matter (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). Matter stays conserved even when we divide something a second time.\n\nDividing again simply means repeating an action we have already performed. It is the same idea we met when we studied multiplication (<htcite2>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "68227419-2430-4359-b902-f6cf87b8941d",
                     "text": "Holmes, F. L.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1985",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7cc05340-732f-4c3d-b260-5437670fbc99",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Shared Origin",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: The Next Step</b><br /><br />Based on what we have just read, imagine a sheet of paper that has already been divided into two parts. Now divide each of those two parts by 2 again.<br /><br />How would you represent this new division? Draw it on a sheet of paper and compare the result with the two parts you started with.</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_exercise",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Exercising\n\nOur goal is to obtain four equal pieces from one sheet of paper.\n\nTo make the idea clear, we will go step by step: first we will look at what happens, and then we will write down what we are doing — just as we did when we divided the sheet into two halves (<htcite0>).\n\n#### The Half\n\nThe first step is simple: choose one whole sheet, like the one in Table 1.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Whole sheet</th></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\" style=\"height: 300px;\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Representation of a blank sheet.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 2: Fractioning</b><br /><br />1. Fold a sheet in half.<br />2. Now, open it and cut along the fold.</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Divided sheet</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: Sheet divided into two pieces.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Finally, we write this division as a fraction: the numerator (the top number) shows the whole we started with, and the denominator (the bottom number) shows how many equal parts we obtained.\n\nIf you do not remember how we arrived at this result, review the text where we explained this process (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Representation of two separate parts.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Dividing Again\n\nIn this stage we will repeat **Practice 2**, but this time we will divide each half into two equal pieces:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\">Divided sheet</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 3</b>: Sheet divided into four pieces.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### What Did We Do?\n\nLet us describe what we are about to do.\n\nWe already have two equal pieces, and we want to divide each of them into two again.\n\nWe can write this goal as:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Our goal.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: I’ve Done Something Similar Before</b><br /><br />Our first division gave us <b>Calculation 1</b>.<br /><br />Using <b>Formula 1</b>, how can we represent what we see in <b>Table 3</b>?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We are repeating the same process: we divided once, and now we will divide **again**.\n\nWe have already seen that repeating an addition is the same as multiplying (<htcite0>).\n\nWith that in mind, reflect:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: Next Step</b><br /><br />If we’re repeating steps, what would happen if we multiplied <b>Formula 1</b> on both sides of <b>Calculation 1</b>?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Because **Calculation 1** is an equality, and matter must be conserved, we cannot change only one side. Therefore, we will multiply every number on both sides by **Formula 1**.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mn>1</mn><mn>)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Multiplication representing the process of a new division.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In **Calculation 2**, the left-hand side multiplies a number by a fraction, while the right-hand side multiplies fractions by fractions.\n\nWe can make the formula uniform by writing the number 1 as a fraction in which the whole is divided by the whole:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 3</b>: Multiplication representing the new division process, with all numbers written as fractions.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now that everything is written as a fraction, we can multiply them: numerators with numerators, and denominators with denominators (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0836cb37-3574-4ca8-9efa-d3fd158b0805",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Fractions",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>2 × 1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2 × 1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2 × 1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2 × 2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2 × 1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2 × 2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 4</b>: Result of fraction multiplication.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Next, we simplify the left side of **Calculation 4**: the fraction 2/2 equals the whole (1).\n\nOn the right side, we split each numerator into two equal parts (1 + 1 = 2):",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 5</b>: Representation of Table 3.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "With **Calculation 5** we represent exactly what we see in **Table 3**: four equal pieces add up to one whole sheet.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nUsing what we already knew about addition and multiplication, we saw in this text that dividing again can be derived from those earlier ideas by multiplying fractions.\n\nIf, for any reason, you did not notice that we were dividing at every step, do not worry: the important point is that each new operation grows out of the ones we already know.\n\nThis knowledge is one more step in building our understanding of the universe, because it underpins many fields — from counting the money we spend on food to the principles that help us explain the universe itself, which we will revisit in the next text.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "In a fraction, does the numerator (the top number) represent the whole?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "The numerator (the top number) represents the whole that is being divided, while the denominator (the bottom number) tells into how many equal parts that whole is split."
      },
      {
         "question": "When we multiply fractions, do we multiply the whole (numerator) by the whole and the parts (denominator) by the parts?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "When we multiply fractions, we multiply numerators with numerators and denominators with denominators — in other words, like with like."
      },
      {
         "question": "Let us return to Calculation 1. Suppose we now divide each half into 3 equal parts. Does this mean that, in the end, we will have 3 parts of 1/3?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><br /><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mn>1</mn><mn>)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>(</mn><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mn>)</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac mathsize=\"big\"><mrow><mn>3 × 1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3 × 1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3 × 1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3 × 2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3 × 1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3 × 2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac